.TH GREP 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
grep \- print lines that match a pattern
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grep
.RB [ \-ivnclqhHFE ]
.I pattern
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B grep
.RB [ \-ivnclqhHFE ]
.B \-e
.I pattern
.RB [ \-e
.IR pattern ]...
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B grep
reads each
.I file
and prints the lines that match any
.IR pattern .
With no
.IR file ,
or where a
.I file
is
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
With more than one file, each line printed starts with its file's name
and a colon.
.PP
A
.I pattern
is a POSIX basic regular expression (BRE), or with
.B \-E
an extended one (ERE), as far as Lua's patterns can express them:
.TP
.B .
any character
.TP
.B *
the item before it, any number of times
.TP
.BR ^ " and " $
the start of the line (first in the pattern) and its end (last)
.TP
.BR [...] " and " [^...]
a set of characters, or any character not in it; ranges such as
.B a\-z
and the classes
.BR [:alpha:] ,
.BR [:digit:] ,
.BR [:alnum:] ,
.BR [:space:] ,
.BR [:upper:] ,
.BR [:lower:] ,
.BR [:punct:] ,
.B [:xdigit:]
and
.B [:cntrl:]
work inside it
.TP
.BI \e c
the character
.I c
itself
.TP
.BR + " and " ?
with
.B \-E
only: one or more, zero or one of the item before; in a BRE they are
plain characters
.PP
Groups, alternation and intervals (\fB\e( \e)\fR, \fB\e|\fR,
\fB\e{n,m\e}\fR, or with
.B \-E
\fB( ) | { }\fR) are not supported: such a pattern is refused with a
message.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-e " pattern"
Use
.I pattern
as a pattern; may be given more than once.
Every argument that is not an option is then a
.IR file .
.TP
.B \-E
Patterns are extended regular expressions.
.TP
.B \-F
Patterns are fixed strings, matched as they are.
.TP
.B \-i
Ignore case.
.TP
.B \-v
Select the lines that do not match.
.TP
.B \-n
Put each line's number (and a colon) before it.
.TP
.B \-c
Print only a count of the selected lines, for each file.
.TP
.B \-l
Print only the names of the files with a selected line
(\fB(standard input)\fR for standard input).
.TP
.B \-q
Print nothing; exit 0 at the first selected line.
.TP
.B \-h
Never put file names before lines.
.TP
.B \-H
Always put file names before lines.
.TP
.B \-\-
The arguments after it are all files.
.PP
Single\-letter options may be combined, as in
.BR \-in .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if a line was selected, 1 if none was, 2 for an error (a bad
option or pattern, or a file that could not be opened), unless
.B \-q
found a line.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
grep \-n require /bin/ls
grep \-ic "^local" *.lua
ps | grep \-v sh
grep \-E "ab+c?" notes.txt
.fi
.SH NOTES
There are no context options (\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-B\fR, \fB\-C\fR), no
.B \-r
and no
.BR \-o .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR find (1),
.BR head (1),
.BR tail (1),
.BR sh (1)
